            <ArticleTitle>A Giant Mature Cystic Teratoma Mimicking a Pleural Effusion.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1259175</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2016/1259175</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The vast majority of teratomas originating from more than a single germ layer are benign. Often, such teratomas are initially asymptomatic. Later symptoms are caused by the weight per se of the teratoma and include chest pain, cough, dyspnea, and/or recurrent attacks of pneumonia. A mediastinal teratoma is treated by total surgical resection of the mass. Here, we report a case of giant mature cystic teratoma mimicking a pleural effusion in the thorax at the 7-month-old female patient with a symptom of persistent pulmonary infection and tachypnea.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
